# --- Godot + C# (client/) ---
#
# Godot editor metadata policy (Godot 4.4+ best practice):
#   - TRACK *.uid files: stable resource identifiers for scripts and scenes.
#     Godot 4.4+ relies on these for cross-resource references; without them,
#     scenes break when files are moved or renamed across machines.
#   - TRACK individual *.import files: per-asset import metadata (one sibling
#     file per source asset, e.g. icon.svg.import). Required so every clone
#     imports assets the same way.
#   - IGNORE the .godot/ and .import/ directories: these are local editor
#     caches (rebuildable on first editor open) and contain machine-specific
#     paths plus the imported binary blobs. They must never be committed.
#   - IGNORE bin/, obj/, *.user, *.suo: standard .NET / IDE local state.
#   - IGNORE exports/, builds/, *.pck, *.translation: build outputs.
client/.godot/
client/.import/
client/bin/
client/obj/
client/tests/bin/
client/tests/obj/
client/*.user
client/*.suo
client/exports/
client/builds/
client/*.pck
client/*.translation

# --- ML model artefacts inside client (Phase 6 L2+) ---
# The encoder ONNX (~45 MB fp16) and tokenizer/vocab files ship as release
# assets, fetched manually into client/ml/ before running the test suite.
# Same discipline as the top-level artifacts/ folder. Commit the C# source
# and the small derived parity fixture (parity/), never the binary blobs.
#
# Exception (Phase 6 L4): the resolution-head ONNX (~3 MB fp32) IS committed
# under client/ml/. Asymmetry is size-driven, not aesthetic -- the head is
# small enough to ship with the source (no release-asset fetch step on a
# fresh clone) and is the contract artefact OnnxClassifier composes with the
# encoder. The encoder stays out (45 MB blows the repo).
client/ml/*.onnx
!client/ml/resolution-head-v0.1.onnx
client/ml/tokenizer.json
client/ml/vocab.txt
